WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:08.411
Film Courage: When you go back into the mainstream
population (for lack of a better word) and

00:00:08.411 --> 00:00:14.740
you see people who come to classes or whatever
and they challenge different beliefs about

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.660
story structure or writing or the need to
take classes or whatever.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.970
Do you see they are justified in being angry
about certain things?

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:29.029
Because you can look at how angry people get
over something so innocent as screenwriting

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:31.810
and you say “What’s the justification
for this?”

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:33.410
You can take it or leave it.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.300
Take what you like, leave the rest kind of
thing.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.860
Some people become so dogmatic about certain
things and it almost becomes a fight about

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.750
religion when it’s just screenwriting.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:49.830
Alan Watt, Author, Screenwriter and Founder
of L.A. Writers' Lab: Right well those kind

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:53.500
of people don’t stay very long in a class.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:01:02.211
And I also have no desire to argue with people
or…that’s not true.

00:01:02.211 --> 00:01:07.750
My interest as a writing teacher is to get
to the truth.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:14.570
So if someone is arguing with me about something
or is staunchly holding a position, my question

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:15.830
is “Is that true?

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:16.830
Is what you’re saying true?

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:17.830
Let’s explore that.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:18.830
Let’s examine it.”

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.250
Because I have no dog in this race.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:25.520
I’m not dogmatic at all.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.480
I have been teaching a long time.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.180
I’ve been writing a long time.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:39.290
I’ve got a method that I’m teaching people
but I don’t know if I would actually call

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:51.390
it a method…process that I’m teaching
but yeah I don’t get into fights with people

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:52.390
about writing.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:58.530
It’s like you believe that and you’re
so staunchly holding that point of view well

00:01:58.530 --> 00:01:59.530
then good.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:03.030
How’s it working for you?

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.870
Is it helping you with your writing?

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:10.120
If it is then great, and if it’s not then…

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:12.310
I see it a lot with writing teachers.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:18.480
I think there are a lot of writing teachers
who are…it’s almost like they’ve got

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:28.730
to secure their sort of place in the world
issuing everything that isn’t what they

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:37.190
teach and that can be I’ve seen it a lot
where the latest thing is that some writing

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:49.160
teachers say the three-act structure is dead
it’s, it’s remedial, it’s not going

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:54.040
to…it’s going to squash any creativity.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:03:00.670
The problem with that is that they don’t
understand what the three-act structure is

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:09.860
because the three-act structure has nothing
to do with plotting in my opinion and unfortunately

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:16.860
a lot of story structure is taught by story
analysts who are sort of brilliant left brain-ers

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:26.180
and are really adept at deconstructing a masterpiece
and sort of the implication is that now we’ve

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:29.590
deconstructed this you should go off and write
your masterpiece.

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:41.190
And while there’s tremendous amount of value
in studying film theory or…film theory because

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:54.940
it’s deconstructing CASABLANCA or CITIZEN
KANE is going to help you understand how a

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.810
story was built to some degree.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:00.200
But it’s not teach you process.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:04.090
It’s not going to teach you to organize
your ideas.

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:09.760
When you look at a movie like CASABLANCA and
you think about the Epstein Brothers writing

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:17.109
or structuring this movie I’m interested
in the process.

00:04:17.109 --> 00:04:20.849
I’m interested in how did somebody come
to write this.

00:04:20.849 --> 00:04:27.199
In other words you can sort of like just because
you deconstruct something so it’s like vivisection

00:04:27.199 --> 00:04:35.070
you can’t take something not living and
look at all of its existing parts and then

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:36.070
reanimate it.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:37.819
It’s not going to come back to life.

00:04:37.819 --> 00:04:45.229
So just because you can break down this thing
into its sort of separate parts doesn’t

00:04:45.229 --> 00:04:50.860
mean you are going to uncover the mystery
of what the thing actually is.

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:56.490
In other words story structure is really the
DNA of your protagonists transformation.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:57.780
Does that make sense?

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:00.729
Story structure is really the DNA of your
protagonists transformation.

00:05:00.729 --> 00:05:08.059
It’s not in the midpoint there should be
a reversal.

00:05:08.059 --> 00:05:09.370
Like I don’t even know what that means?

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:15.779
You hear it all the time with these story
analysts that there should be a reversal.

00:05:15.779 --> 00:05:17.470
What the hell does that mean?

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:20.050
Does IT’S A WONDERFUL LIFE have a reversal?

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:25.789
That’s where Mr. Potter offers Jimmy Stewart
a job and Jimmy Stewart says no.

00:05:25.789 --> 00:05:27.430
I don’t know?

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:34.569
I think of story structure as not a conceptual
model something you can figure out but it’s

00:05:34.569 --> 00:05:38.020
more of a experiential model.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:43.629
In other words you protagonist goes through
a series of experiences that lead to a transformation.

00:05:43.629 --> 00:05:50.839
And I think of story structure as an immutable
paradigm for a spiritual transformation.

00:05:50.839 --> 00:05:53.819
It’s immutable, it never changes.

00:05:53.819 --> 00:06:00.789
But that doesn’t make it…that doesn’t
limit your creativity.

00:06:00.789 --> 00:06:05.539
That actually paradoxically opens your creativity.

00:06:05.539 --> 00:06:11.599
In other words when you really understand
what story structure is it moves you beyond

00:06:11.599 --> 00:06:13.710
your limited imagination of what your story
is.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:17.119
I always tell my writers your idea of your
story is not the whole story.

00:06:17.119 --> 00:06:20.509
It’s not that it’s incorrect, it’s that
it’s incomplete.

00:06:20.509 --> 00:06:27.539
And so if you were to distill story structure
to three words it would be Desire - Surrender

00:06:27.539 --> 00:06:30.110
- Transformation.

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:34.270
Show me any story, you are going to have a
character that wants something (desire).

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:38.309
You are going to have a character that lets
go of the meaning they made out of their goal

00:06:38.309 --> 00:06:39.309
(surrender).

00:06:39.309 --> 00:06:44.819
You are going to have a character that experiences
transformation in other words a shift in perception.

00:06:44.819 --> 00:06:49.919
A reframing of their relationship to their
goal.

00:06:49.919 --> 00:06:55.999
Show me a story that doesn’t have that and
I’ll show you a story that doesn’t work

00:06:55.999 --> 00:06:59.819
or is really experimental okay.

00:06:59.819 --> 00:07:10.610
But that’s when people…it’s happens
not when I teach so much as if I do a lecture

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:17.030
to a bunch of people there’s always sort
of the contrarian, the person who is like

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:19.040
“You know I don’t agree with that.”

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:22.629
And I’m like “That’s great, let’s
talk about that.”

00:07:22.629 --> 00:07:32.159
But it’s usually about something that they
are trying to work out with themselves and

00:07:32.159 --> 00:07:34.610
it’s not really…I don’t know?

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:42.449
It’s usually that they are looking for some
kind of fight rather than they are actually

00:07:42.449 --> 00:07:47.689
sincerely interested in deepening their process.

